Technical Design Document v1
Infrastructure Tycoon

1. Technical Overview
Infrastructure Tycoon is a 2D business simulation game targeting Unity and C#. The game begins with the player hosting small websites on a single PC and scales into a global cloud infrastructure provider operating racks, datacenters, regions, and AI compute campuses.
The technical design prioritizes:
	• PC-first development
	• Future portability to web and mobile
	• Moderate simulation depth
	• Data-driven content
	• Long-term save scalability
	• Hybrid detailed and abstracted simulation
	• Continuous time with adjustable speed controls

2. Target Platform
Primary Platform
PC
Initial release targets:
	• Windows
	• macOS
	• Linux
	• Steam
Future Platforms
The architecture should avoid hard dependencies that would block:
	• Web export
	• Tablet/mobile ports
	• Console ports
Desktop mouse interaction is the primary input model, but UI systems should be designed so touch interaction can be supported later.

3. Engine & Language
Engine
Unity 2D using Unity 6.6 (6000.6.9f1), the installed supported release selected for this project. This is not an LTS release. ProjectSettings/ProjectVersion.txt records the exact editor version and revision.
Primary Language
C# for simulation, persistence, gameplay, UI, and tests.

4. Core Technical Goals
Scalability
The game must support:
	• A single PC in early game
	• Dozens of devices in mid-game
	• Hundreds of racks in late-game
	• Multiple datacenters and global regions in endgame
	• Named story customers plus thousands of abstract background customers
Readability
The player should understand why things happen.
Failures should be explainable:
	• CPU overloaded
	• Storage full
	• Bandwidth saturated
	• Cooling insufficient
	• Power backup missing
	• SLA breached
Data-Driven Design
Most game content should be stored as data rather than hardcoded logic.
Examples:
	• Hardware definitions
	• Contract templates
	• Customer archetypes
	• Events
	• Research items
	• Facility upgrades
	• Vendor stats
	• Era unlocks
Expandability
The game should be easy to expand with:
	• New contract types
	• New hardware tiers
	• New customer stories
	• New regions
	• New cloud services
	• New failure types

5. High-Level Architecture
The project should be organized around several major systems.
Core Systems
	• GameManager
	• TimeManager
	• EconomyManager
	• SimulationManager
	• InfrastructureManager
	• ContractManager
	• CustomerManager
	• EventManager
	• ResearchManager
	• SaveManager
	• UIManager
Runtime Composition
A GameRoot MonoBehaviour initializes the session and connects plain C# simulation services to scene components. Keep session state and simulation rules in plain C# classes with explicit dependencies. Use MonoBehaviours for input, rendering, and UI bindings. Drive simulation ticks independently of UI updates. This is the MVP architecture. The manager list above describes responsibilities. Introduce separate services as the playable loop requires them.

6. Time System
The game uses continuous time with adjustable speed.
Supported Speeds
	• Paused
	• 1x
	• 2x
	• 4x
	• 8x
Optional later:
	• 16x for late-game fast forwarding
Time Units
The game should track:
	• Days
	• Months
	• Years
MVP Clock Contract
Start January 1, 1995 using the Gregorian calendar. At 1x, one game day takes two real seconds. Preserve fractional days while paused and across speed changes. Process every crossed day in chronological order. At each date boundary, finalize the outgoing day, then the outgoing month if it ended, then the outgoing year if it ended. Bill once for each outgoing month.

Implemented clock API: InfrastructureTycoon.Simulation.SimulationClock is engine-independent. Advance(elapsedSeconds) consumes nonnegative real seconds at the selected SimulationSpeed (default Normal). Date starts at midnight on January 1, 1995; DayProgress exposes the fraction of the current day completed. DayEnded, MonthEnded, and YearEnded carry the outgoing date. During each callback Date is the new day and DayProgress is zero; after Advance returns it includes any remaining fraction. CompanySession subscribes once to MonthEnded and bills each outgoing period once. Hosted contracts receive their full authored monthly revenue without proration; owned hardware incurs its authored recurring expense even with no hosted contracts. The session retains immutable monthly results, processed-period protection, and per-contract paid-month counts. Cash and cumulative operating profit change by the monthly net amount; growth and unlocks remain deferred. GameRoot creates a CompanySession from the authored StarterCatalog. The engine-independent session owns its clock, finances, hardware instances, and initially empty accepted-contract collection. GameRoot advances that same clock with Time.unscaledDeltaTime and binds the date, selected speed, and five HUD buttons. Simulation speed does not change Unity Time.timeScale.

Tick Types
Different systems update at different intervals.
Frame Update
Used for:
	• UI animation
	• visual feedback
	• camera movement
Daily Tick
Used for:
	• workload fluctuations
	• hardware wear
	• satisfaction changes
	• small incidents
Monthly Tick
Used for:
	• billing
	• salaries
	• rent
	• customer growth
	• contract renewal
	• financial reports
Yearly Tick
Used for:
	• industry trends
	• technology shifts
	• major customer milestones
	• market reputation changes

7. Simulation Model
The simulation uses a hybrid detail model.
Early Game: Detailed Simulation
The player manages individual hardware components.
Example:
	• PC
	• CPU
	• RAM
	• Storage
	• Network connection
This creates an intimate startup feeling where every upgrade matters.
Mid Game: Rack Simulation
Once the player reaches server racks, the game begins grouping equipment into rack-level capacity pools.
A rack tracks:
	• CPU capacity
	• RAM capacity
	• Storage capacity
	• Bandwidth capacity
	• Power usage
	• Heat output
	• Reliability
	• Utilization
Late Game: Datacenter Simulation
Large infrastructure is pooled at the datacenter or zone level.
A datacenter tracks:
	• Compute pool
	• Memory pool
	• Storage pool
	• Bandwidth pool
	• Power capacity
	• Cooling capacity
	• Redundancy score
	• Reliability score
Endgame: Region Simulation
Global infrastructure is tracked at regional scale.
A region tracks:
	• Datacenters
	• Availability zones
	• Customer workloads
	• Latency coverage
	• failover capacity
	• market presence

8. Infrastructure Abstraction System
The game should transition from detailed objects to pooled capacity as scale increases.
Example
Early rack:
	• 12 individual servers
	• 2 storage units
	• 1 switch
	• 1 UPS
Later abstraction:
Rack Pool A:
	• 240 CPU
	• 768 GB RAM
	• 100 TB Storage
	• 40 Gbps Bandwidth
	• 97% Reliability
This keeps late-game saves performant while preserving the fantasy of physical infrastructure growth.

