18. UI Architecture
Use a Canvas with a Canvas Scaler and anchored RectTransforms for the HUD and station panels. Keep camera framing independent of UI scaling.
MVP Interaction
The player walks between stations in the garage. Interaction within reach opens the station panel. Use the PC station for contracts and workload assignment and the hardware station for purchases and installation. Keep date, speed, cash, and alerts visible in the HUD.

Main UI Areas
	• top financial bar
	• time controls
	• infrastructure view
	• contract panel
	• customer panel
	• alerts panel
	• research screen
	• company stats screen
	• facility management screen
Critical UI Requirement
Every simulation problem must be explainable.
Example alert:
“CloudCart is experiencing degraded performance because Rack A is at 96% CPU utilization.”

19. Save/Load System
The game needs robust long-term save support.
Save Format
Serialize versioned C# save models as JSON under Application.persistentDataPath. Store stable content IDs and runtime values independently of scene objects. Consider compression if save size becomes an issue.
The first save round trip lands with Phase 2 billing. Persist fractional day progress and stable content IDs. Capture saves between session updates after all crossed boundaries have been processed.

Save Data Includes
	• company state
	• current date
	• money
	• reputation
	• infrastructure
	• contracts
	• customers
	• research
	• events
	• facilities
	• world progression
	• unlocked eras
Save Versioning
Every save file should include:
	• save_version
	• game_version
	• timestamp
This allows future migrations when systems change.

20. Data-Driven Content Pipeline
Use ScriptableObject assets for authored definitions and JSON or CSV for external catalogs where useful. Keep mutable state in C# runtime models.
Data Definitions
	• HardwareDefinition
	• ContractTemplate
	• CustomerArchetype
	• EventDefinition
	• ResearchDefinition
	• FacilityUpgradeDefinition
	• VendorDefinition
	• EraDefinition
This makes balancing easier and supports future modding.

21. Performance Strategy
Key Principle
Do not simulate every object forever.
Use detail where it matters and abstraction where scale demands it.
Early Game
Simulate individual hardware.
Mid Game
Simulate racks and pools.
Late Game
Simulate datacenters and regions.
Background Customers
Aggregate into groups rather than full entities.
Story Customers
Remain detailed and persistent.

22. Scene Structure
Use a Main.unity scene with a GameRoot, an orthographic camera, a WorldView root, and a UI Canvas. Build the garage and later infrastructure views as prefabs. Use separate Collider2D blockers and interaction triggers so station reach and floor clearance can be tuned independently.

23. Code Architecture
Suggested Unity project layout:

- Assets/Scenes for playable scenes
- Assets/Prefabs for world and UI prefabs
- Assets/Scripts for C# gameplay, simulation, persistence, and presentation
- Assets/Data for authored definitions and catalogs
- Assets/Tests/EditMode and Assets/Tests/PlayMode for automated coverage
- Packages and ProjectSettings for dependencies and editor configuration

24. Testing Strategy
Use the Unity Test Framework. Run Edit Mode tests for simulation rules and save round trips. Run Play Mode tests for scene interactions, collision, camera framing, and responsive UI. Run both suites from the Test Runner or in batch mode for CI.

Unit Tests
Test:
	• resource allocation
	• contract validation
	• billing calculations
	• failure probability
	• customer growth
	• save/load serialization
Simulation Tests
Run long-term automated saves to verify:
	• economy stability
	• no runaway debt loops
	• no impossible progression blocks
	• late-game performance remains acceptable
Balance Tests
Check:
	• contract rewards
	• hardware costs
	• research pacing
	• outage frequency
	• customer growth rates

